const body = `<h1>FRQ 3: Data Analysis with ArrayList</h1>

<p>
    5 points. New in the 2025 revision: this question is ArrayList-ONLY
    (arrays were removed from it). You get a class describing records, a
    populated ArrayList, and a data question. Your 6.6 telemetry work, 7.5
    pattern drills, and E3 day were all rehearsals for this exact question.
</p>

<h2>Round A: Bestiary Census (5 points)</h2>

<div class="callout lab">
<p>A <code>Monster</code> class is given:</p>
<pre><code>public class Monster {
    public String getName()       // e.g. "goblin"
    public String getType()       // "slime", "undead", "brute", "boss"
    public int getHp()            // current hit points
    public int getGold()          // gold dropped
}</code></pre>
<p>A game maintains <code>ArrayList&lt;Monster&gt; bestiary</code>. Write the
method:</p>
<pre><code>/** Returns the total gold dropped by all monsters of the given
 *  type whose hp is at least minHp.
 */
public int goldByType(String type, int minHp)</code></pre>
<p>Examples: goldByType("boss", 20) with a lich (450 gold, 40 hp) and dragon
(500 gold, 60 hp) returns 950. goldByType("boss", 50) returns 500 (the lich
is excluded). A type with no matches returns 0.</p>
</div>

<h3>Solution</h3>
<pre><code>public int goldByType(String type, int minHp) {
    int total = 0;
    for (Monster m : bestiary) {
        if (m.getType().equals(type) &amp;&amp; m.getHp() &gt;= minHp) {
            total += m.getGold();
        }
    }
    return total;
}</code></pre>

<p>Commentary: it is pattern 4 (conditional aggregate) from the datasets
page, wearing a costume. Two conditions in the filter, one accumulator, cast
free because everything is int. The whole question is reading the filter
correctly: "of the given type" AND "hp at least minHp." equals for the
String, &gt;= for the hp.</p>

<h3>Rubric (5 points)</h3>
<table>
    <tr><th>Point</th><th>Earned by</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Traverse the ArrayList (any correct loop form)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>String comparison with .equals (== loses this point
        and usually the next)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Both filter conditions present and correctly combined</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Accumulation into a total, initialized to 0</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>Return the total (not print), correct on empty
        matches</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="roundb">Round B: Quit Analysis (mock material)</h2>

<div class="callout lab">
<p>A <code>PlayerRecord</code> class is given with
<code>getClassType()</code> ("Warrior", "Mage", "Rogue"),
<code>getLevel()</code>, <code>getDeaths()</code>, and
<code>didQuit()</code> (true if the player quit). A dataset
<code>ArrayList&lt;PlayerRecord&gt; telemetry</code> is populated.</p>
<pre><code>/** Returns the average level of players who did quit, as a
 *  double. Returns 0.0 when no players quit.
 */
public double averageQuitLevel()

/** Returns the name of the class type with the most deaths across
 *  all players (quit or not). Ties: first in the order Warrior,
 *  Mage, Rogue.
 */
public String mostDoomedClass()</code></pre>
<p>Watch: division by zero guard, double cast on the average, and the tie
    rule. The tie rule is not decoration, it is a rubric row.</p>
</div>

<h2>The Four Patterns, One Question</h2>

<p>Almost every FRQ 3 ever written is one or two of the four aggregation
patterns (see the <a href="../../docs/unit-06/datasets.html">datasets
page</a>): total/mean, count-by-category, max-and-who, conditional aggregate.
Before writing code, name the pattern out loud. "This is conditional
aggregate." "This is max-and-who with a tie rule." Naming the pattern tells
you the loop shape in two seconds.</p>

<table>
    <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Loop shape</th><th>Classic trap</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Total / mean</td><td>accumulator + counter</td><td>int division:
        cast to double; count-0 guard</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Count by category</td><td>counter(s) under an if</td><td>overlapping
        conditions counting one record twice</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Max and who</td><td>best value + best index/object</td><td>ties:
        read the tie rule from the spec</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Conditional aggregate</td><td>acc + counter inside the if</td>
        <td>denominator counting ALL rows</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Drill List</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Write goldByType from memory. Then rewrite it with an index loop
        instead of for-each. Both forms must be automatic.</li>
    <li>equals vs == on Strings: one line each, know which earns the point.</li>
    <li>The (double) cast on averages: write averageQuitLevel's division line
        three different correct ways.</li>
</ul>`;
